// PSS v3.0 LRM — Example 180: Value resolution (page 248)
struct s {
rand bit[3] p1;        // type expresses values in the range  0 to 7
int [3]     p2;        // type expresses values in the range -4 to 3
covergroup {
coverpoint p1 {
bins b1 = [1, 2..5, 6..10]; // warning issued for range 6..10
bins b2 = [-1, 1..10, 15];  // warning issued for range 1..10
}
                            //           and values -1 and 15
coverpoint p2 {
bins b3 = [1, 2..5, 6..10]; // warning issued for ranges 2..5
                            //                       and 6..10
bins b4 = [-1, 1..10, 15];  // warning issued for range  1..10
}
                            //                    and value 15
} c1;
}

18.4 Defining cross coverage
A covergroup can specify cross coverage between two or more coverage points or variables. Cross
coverage is specified using the cross construct (see Syntax 67). When a variable V is part of a cross
coverage, the PSS processing tool shall implicitly create a coverage point for the variable, as if it had been
created by the statement coverpoint V;. Thus, a cross involves only coverage points. Expressions
cannot be used directly in a cross; a coverage point must be explicitly defined first.
18.4.1 Syntax
Syntax 67 declares a cross.
Syntax 67—cross declaration
